    "text_blocks": [
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "Spotting hail damage on a roof requires checking for dark circular pockmarks, missing granules, and soft bruises on shingles, as well as looking for dents on nearby metal vents and gutters.",
        "snippet_highlighted_words": [
          "checking for dark circular pockmarks, missing granules, and soft bruises on shingles, as well as looking for dents on nearby metal vents and gutters"
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Ground-Level Signs"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Check soft metals: Look for dings or dents on your gutters, downspouts, flashing, AC units, and window frames. If these metal items are dented, your roof likely took hits too."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Inspect the gutters: Look inside your gutters or near downspout exits for an unusual buildup of sandy shingle granules."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Scan siding and garage doors: Look for small chips, dimples, or paint marks on the sides of your home."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "Learn how to spot hidden dents on metal surfaces and other exterior clues around your home:"
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Roof-Level Signs (Asphalt Shingles)"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Dark impact spots: Look for round, speckled, or dark spots where hailstones knocked off the protective outer layer of granules. Lighter-colored shingles make these marks easier to spot."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Soft or bruised spots: Press gently on the shingles. A hail impact can bruise or crush the underlying fiberglass mat, leaving a soft or spongy feeling under your finger."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Exposed fibers: Severe hail can strip granules completely away, exposing the dark asphalt or shiny fiberglass mat underneath."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "See a close-up example of how a hail strike fractures a shingle's mat and dislodges granules:"
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Signs on Other Roof Materials"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Tile roofs (clay or concrete): Look for cracked, chipped, or fractured edges along the tiles."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Metal roofs: Look for dimples or indentations, though keep in mind some insurance policies exclude purely cosmetic dents unless functionality is compromised."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "If you suspect damage, would you like advice on how to safely document the roof or when to call an insurance adjuster?"
      }
    ],
